, prepare 41ms)\n\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784980840852},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 4 tests pass. Now I'll proceed with the refactoring process.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1: Naming Evaluation (FIRST PRIORITY)\n\n**Naming Evaluation**:\n- Current name: `nextGeneration`\n- Function purpose: Given an array of live cells, computes the next generation of Conway's Game of Life using the standard rules (underpopulation, survival, overpopulation, reproduction).\n- Question: Does \"nextGeneration\" clearly reveal this intent?\n- Assessment: Yes — `nextGeneration` is idiomatic in Game of Life and precisely describes \"compute the next generation of this grid state.\" It's the standard name used across Game of Life implementations.\n- Internal variable names:\n  - `neighborCounts` — clearly describes a map tracking neighbor counts\n  - `liveSet` — clearly describes a Set of live cell coordinates\n  - `result` — generic but acceptable for a return accumulator\n  - `key` — somewhat generic; could be `coordKey` but the context makes it clear\n  - `count` — clear enough in the context of iterating over neighborCounts\n  - `dx`, `dy` — standard for delta offsets in grid traversal\n\n**Decision**: Keep `nextGeneration` — the name fits perfectly and is the standard naming convention. The internal names are also clear. No naming improvement warranted.\n\n---\n\n## Step 2: Calculate Initial APP Mass\n\n**Current Code Mass**:\n\n```typescript\ntype Cell = [number, number];\n\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  if (cells.length === 0) return [];\n  \n  const neighborCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n  \n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {\n    for (let dx = -1; dx <= 1; dx++) {\n      for (let dy = -1; dy <= 1; dy++) {\n        if (dx === 0 && dy === 0) continue;\n        const key = `${x + dx},${y + dy}`;\n        neighborCounts.set(key, (neighborCounts.get(key) || 0) + 1);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  \n  const result: Cell[] = [];\n  const liveSet = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => `${x},${y}`));\n  \n  for (const [key, count] of neighborCounts) {\n    const [x, y] = key.split(\",\").map(Number) as [number, number];\n    \n    if (count === 3 || (count === 2 && liveSet.has(key))) {\n      result.push([x, y]);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nComponent Count:\n- **Constants**: 7 (`0` x4 in `cells.length === 0`, `dx = -1`, `dx <= 1`, `dy = -1`, `dy <= 1`, `dx === 0 && dy === 0`, `0` in `|| 0`, `\"0\"` in template literal, `\",\"` x2, `Number`, `3`, `2`) — let me count carefully:\n  - `0` in `cells.length === 0`: 1\n  - `-1` in `dx = -1`: 1\n  - `-1` in `dx = -1`: 1 (same literal, counted once if unique... actually each occurrence is distinct in APP): 1\n  - `1` in `dx <= 1`: 1\n  - `-1` in `dy = -1`: 1\n  - `1` in `dy <= 1`: 1\n  - `0` in `dx === 0`: 1\n  - `0` in `dy === 0`: 1\n  - `\"0\"` (the string in template): actually that's part of the template literal... let me be more precise.\n  \n  Let me count each distinct occurrence of a literal value:\n  - `0` (in `cells.length === 0`) — constant\n  - `-1` (in `dx = -1`) — constant\n  - `1` (in `dx <= 1`) — constant\n  - `-1` (in `dy = -1`) — constant\n  - `1` (in `dy <= 1`) — constant\n  - `0` (in `dx === 0`) — constant\n  - `0` (in `dy === 0`) — constant\n  - `0` in `|| 0` — constant\n  - `\",\"` in template — constant\n  - `\",\"` in `key.split(\",\")` — constant\n  - `\",\"` in liveSet template — constant\n  - `\",\"` in result `[x, y]` — no, those aren't literals\n  \n  Actually, I need to count more carefully. Let me enumerate each literal:\n  - `0` (early return check): 1\n  - `-1` (dx init): 1\n  - `1` (dx bound): 1\n  - `-1` (dy init): 1\n  - `1` (dy bound): 1\n  - `0` (dx === 0): 1\n  - `0` (dy === 0): 1\n  - `0` (|| 0 fallback): 1\n  - `\",\"` (in template literal `${x + dx},${y + dy}`): 1\n  - `\",\"` (in `key.split(\",\")`): 1\n  - `\",\"` (in liveSet template): 1\n  - `Number` (function reference, treated as constant): 1\n  - `3` (count === 3): 1\n  - `2` (count === 2): 1\n  \n  Total constants: 14 × 1 = **14**\n\n- **Bindings**: \n  - `cells` (parameter): 1\n  - `neighborCounts`: 1\n  - `x` (destructured in first loop): 1\n  - `y` (destructured in first loop): 1\n  - `dx`: 1\n  - `dy`: 1\n  - `key` (in neighbor loop): 1\n  - `result`: 1\n  - `liveSet`: 1\n  - `x` (destructured in second loop — different scope): 1\n  - `y` (destructured in second loop): 1\n  - `key` (destructured from neighborCounts): 1\n  - `count` (destructured from neighborCounts): 1\n  \n  Total bindings: 13 × 1 = **13**\n\n- **Invocations**: \n  - `new Map()`: 1\n  - `neighborCounts.set()`: 3 iterations but the function call itself is 1 invocation per occurrence... Actually APP counts each syntactic occurrence, not runtime. Let me recount:\n    - `new Map()`: 1\n    - `neighborCounts.get(key)`: 1\n    - `neighborCounts.set(key, ...)`: 1\n    - `Math...` — no\n    - `new Set()`: 1\n    - `cells.map(...)`: 1\n    - `([x, y]) => ...` arrow: 1\n    - `key.split(\",\")`: 1\n    - `.map(Number)`: 1\n    - `liveSet.has(key)`: 1\n    - `result.push(...)`: 1\n    \n  Wait, I need to think about what counts as an invocation. Method calls, function calls, constructors, operators... The `+` operator in `${x + dx}` — is that an invocation? In the APP spec, `+` was counted as an invocation (mass 2) in the example with `numbers.reduce((sum, num) => sum + num, 0)`. Let me reconsider.\n\n  APP from the spec: \"Invocation (Mass: 2): Function calls (calculate(), Math.max())\"\n\n  The operator `+` should not count as an invocation — it's a built-in operator, not a function call. In the example, `reduce` is the invocation (mass 2), and `+` was likely miscounted or counted as part of the reduce call. Let me stick strictly to the definition: function calls and method calls.\n\n  Invocations:\n  - `new Map()`: 2\n  - `neighborCounts.set(key, ...)`: 2\n  - `neighborCounts.get(key)`: 2\n  - `new Set()`: 2\n  - `cells.map(...)`: 2\n  - `key.